## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Classification

Your code contains three patterns:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `ProcessLoop` | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| ThreadAbortException as control flow | `catch` block | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Thread.ResetAbort | Inside catch | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` and continue |

### Why This Breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — the runtime will **never** inject a `ThreadAbortException`. Your `Stop()` method will crash, and the thread will hang indefinitely if `GetNextItem()` blocks.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| **`GetNextItem()` blocks forever** | You *must* pass the token into blocking calls. If it uses `BlockingCollection<T>`, use `.Take(token)`. If it uses `WaitHandle`, use `WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { handle, token.WaitHandle })`. |
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` between expensive steps. |
| **Token not passed through call chain** | Every method that can block must accept and forward the `CancellationToken`. One missing link = hang on shutdown. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Implement `IDisposable` as shown. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | The `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not unrelated ones. |

### If `GetNextItem` Cannot Accept a Token

If it wraps unmodifiable blocking I/O (e.g., legacy socket read), move it to a child process and use `Process.Kill()` as a last resort, or use a timeout wrapper:

```csharp
// Timeout wrapper for uncooperative blocking calls
var getItemTask = Task.Run(() => GetNextItem());
var completed = await getItemTask.WaitAsync(token); // throws OCE on cancel
```

Note: `WaitAsync` abandons the task but doesn't stop the blocked thread — acceptable for shutdown scenarios.
